Interface: pure contract          Abstract class: contract + shared code
 Notifiable.notify()               BaseRepository
  User, Vendor, Rider:              ├ save(), find() written once
  unrelated, all implement          └ map_row(): subclass fills in

Python:
  class BaseRepo(ABC):
      def save(self, x): ...          # shared skeleton
      @abstractmethod
      def map_row(self, row): ...     # subclass hook (template method)

Go:
  type Notifiable interface { Notify(msg string) }   // contract only
  // no abstract classes in Go: share code by embedding a base struct

Real world: Comparable is an interface because String and Integer are unrelated yet both sortable. Django's Model is the abstract-class side: shared save and query machinery, you only declare fields.

HTTP handler ─▶ UserService ─▶ UserRepository (interface)
                                       ▲ implements
                            PostgresUserRepo (infrastructure layer)

Python:
  class UserService:
      def __init__(self, repo): self.repo = repo   # domain sees the interface only

Go:
  type UserRepository interface { Find(id int) (User, error) }
  // postgres.UserRepo satisfies it from the infra package

Real world: moving Postgres to MongoDB = one new repository implementation, UserService untouched. Unit tests run the service with an in-memory repo: no DB container. Framework upgrades stay at the edges so domain code doesn't churn.
